    <h1>How to Convert MPG to Litres per 100 km</h1>
    <p class="lead">Car fuel economy is written in different units around the world. Converting to litres per 100 km lets you compare cars fairly, because a lower figure always means less fuel used.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>The formulas</h2>
<p>For US miles per gallon, litres per 100 km = 235.2 ÷ mpg. For UK miles per gallon, it is 282.5 ÷ mpg. A car that does 30 US mpg uses about 7.84 litres per 100 km.</p>
<h2>Why UK and US figures differ</h2>
<p>A UK gallon is larger than a US gallon, about 4.55 litres against 3.79 litres. The same car therefore shows a higher mpg figure in the UK, even though it uses the same fuel.</p>
<h2>Comparing cars</h2>
<ul>
<li>Compare cars in the same unit, and check whether a figure is a test result or a real-world estimate.</li>
<li>Because the scale is reversed, a drop from 8 to 7 litres per 100 km and a drop from 12 to 11 both save one litre per 100 km. The first is a bigger percentage improvement, because it starts from a lower figure.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/fuel-economy-converter.html">Fuel Economy Converter</a> converts between US mpg, UK mpg and litres per 100 km as you type.</p>
